  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nAn upper trough is currently sliding across the Upper Mississippi \nValley into the Great Lakes as an upper low spins over the \nnorthwestern US with ridging between these two features. At the \nsurface, a front is over northern Oklahoma and is starting to move \nnorth. In our area, persistent mid-level WAA has resulted in \nisolated showers and storms across south-central and southeast KS. \nIsolated chances will remain possible in southeast KS for late this \nafternoon into the early evening. Severe weather looks unlikely with \nthis activity given effective shear is around 20-25 kts with mid-\nlevel lapse rates around 6 C/km, though some gusty winds and brief \nheavy rainfall is possible given DCAPE values around 1000 J/kg and \nPWATs around 1.85\" which is greater than the 75th percentile for \nthis time of year. As we move into tonight, storm chances will \ndecrease with our area remaining mostly dry overnight. Storm chances \nwill stay well off to our northeast as we continue into Monday \nmorning. \n  \nFor Monday, the upper trough over the northwestern CONUS will \ntraverse into the Northern Plains by Monday afternoon. This will \nstrengthen the pressure gradient in the Central Plains, increasing \nlow-level southerly flow. Breezy winds are expected across much of \nour area with sustained winds between 20-30 mph and gusts up to 35-\n45 mph. The strongest winds are likely in central KS where a Wind \nAdvisory may be needed. Hot conditions are expected areawide as well \nabove normal 850mb temperatures are forecast. High temperatures will \nbe in the mid 90s to around 100 with the warmest temperatures along \nand west of I-135. The aforementioned upper trough will push a slow \nmoving cold front into southern Nebraska by late Monday afternoon. \nShower and storm chances will be possible along and ahead of the \nfront as 850mb WAA increases, increasing chances in central KS \ntowards Monday evening and night. Severe storms are most likely \ngoing to stay north of our area Monday night into Tuesday where \nbetter wind shear will be, though a rogue strong gusty wind cannot \nbe ruled out in Russell or Lincoln County; however, chances remain \nvery low. \n\nThe front is progged to stall out across Highway 50 on Tuesday with \nbreezy northeasterly winds behind the front, affecting central KS. \nThis front will also result in a spread of temperatures on Tuesday \nafternoon with highs in the 70s in northern KS to the mid 90s in \nsouthern KS. Instability will increase along and south of the \nboundary, increasing storm chances for much of eastern Kansas on \nTuesday afternoon into the night. Marginal severe storms may be \npossible with effective shear around 30 kts. Heavy rainfall is \nlikely with any activity as PWATs approach the maximum value for \nthis time of year leading to potential flooding concerns especially \nacross central KS and portions of south-central. The frontal \nboundary is likely to stay relatively in the same location for most \nof the upcoming week. Additional weak upper shortwaves will slide \nacross the region, resulting in multiple rounds of possible storm \nchances especially in central KS. For temperatures, warm conditions \nare forecast to continue with highs in the 80s and 90s on Wednesday \nand Thursday. Cooler temperatures are anticipated to arrive as we \nmove into next weekend.",
